Category: Canonicalization
Purpose:
  Defines the canonical concept structure for the knowledge graph.
  Ensures every concept has a single canonical form with aliases,
  related concepts, and typed relations. Prevents knowledge dump
  by enforcing normalization before storage.

Owns: ./

Canonical Structure:
  Every concept MUST have:
    - concept_id: @concept.<name>
    - canonical_name: human-readable preferred term
    - definition: single-sentence description
    - aliases: [alternative names that mean the same]
    - related: [nearby concepts that are NOT the same]
    - relation: typed edge to related concepts (see RELATIONSHIP-VOCABULARY.sdd)

  Example:
    @concept.idempotency
      canonical_name: Idempotency
      definition: Producing the same result regardless of execution count.
      aliases:
        - duplicate protection
        - retry-safe operation
        - duplicate request prevention
      related:
        - @concept.exactly-once
      relation:
        @concept.exactly-once: different-but-related

  Key Distinction:
    aliases == same meaning, different words
    related  == different meaning, semantic proximity

  semantic_similarity != semantic_equality

Relationship Vocabulary:
  same-as         — identical meaning, merge candidates
  alias-of        — synonym, canonical form preferred
  extends         — adds behavior to existing concept
  specializes     — narrower form of a broader concept
  implements      — concrete realization of abstract concept
  depends-on      — requires another concept to function
  related-to      — semantic proximity, not equivalence
  conflicts-with  — mutually exclusive in same context
  replaces        — successor concept, old form deprecated
  derived-from    — originated from another concept
  observed-in     — detected in specific project/context
  validated-by    — confirmed by evidence or decision

Concept Merging Rules:
  [M1] Two concepts with >90% semantic overlap MUST be merge candidates.
  [M2] AI MUST NOT auto-merge — only propose candidate merges.
  [M3] Merge proposals require human approval.
  [M4] On merge, the surviving concept absorbs aliases of the deprecated one.
  [M5] Deprecated concept status changes to "replaced" with pointer to canonical.
  [M6] Merged concepts retain history in versioning log.

Candidate Merge Protocol:
  1. AI detects semantic overlap between candidates.
  2. AI generates merge proposal:
     - candidate_a: <id>
     - candidate_b: <id>
     - overlap_score: <percentage>
     - suggested_canonical: <proposed concept_id>
     - reason: <why merge is recommended>
  3. Human reviews and approves/rejects.
  4. On approval: execute merge, update aliases, deprecate redundant concept.
  5. On rejection: keep separate, record reason in REJECTED-APPROACHES.sdd.

  Example:
    Possible merge:
      A: @candidate.retry-safety
      B: @candidate.retry-consistency
    Suggested canonical: @concept.retry-safety
    Reason: B is mostly a consequence of A.

Anti-Chaos Rule:
  Don't create knowledge when a link is enough.

  Before creating ANY new concept, AI MUST verify:
    - Can existing concept solve this?
    - Can existing reference describe this?
    - Can this be an alias?
    - Can this be a specialization?

  Only if ALL answers are NO, create new knowledge.

